#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64a5605c-aa98-5978-817a-5285ee36d985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286796} {:answer/id #uuid "012d6857-bb46-5de4-8684-4ab56951deb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "012d6857-bb46-5de4-8684-4ab56951deb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286797} :answer-field/id #uuid "a618b2df-588f-5643-a1f7-d50a86ab5784" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75884ce4-b305-5baa-98de-295bb8095922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75884ce4-b305-5baa-98de-295bb8095922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286799} :answer-field/id #uuid "51ffc596-59c2-55b7-829d-d1e530030f06" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87a9eaa7-fb61-5cc5-b62c-3eb19aaa782e" :question/math-academy-id "q-264772" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x}+4x\\cdot {3}^{x}-48\\cdot {3}^{x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x}+4x\\cdot {3}^{x}-48\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+4x-48){3}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+4x-48=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+4x-48=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}+2x-24) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+6)(x-4) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,4.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${3}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-6\\,\\text{and}\\,x=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ac81e57-f612-5fe7-82f7-86ccd0544642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ac81e57-f612-5fe7-82f7-86ccd0544642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286802} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f41be4-3aaa-5694-9690-776a6816a401" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286801} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62262cdb-bdd9-51cb-85b6-74ac22f72586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62262cdb-bdd9-51cb-85b6-74ac22f72586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286804} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7cf90c0-a1f5-591d-95c7-95dbf4c7eb69" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d59ce66f-0016-5927-abf5-0562d6ed9edd" :question/math-academy-id "q-264780" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+11x\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+28\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+11x\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+28\\cdot {5}^{2x+1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+11x+28){5}^{2x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+11x+28=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{2x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+11x+28=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+7)(x+4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-7,-4.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{2x+1}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{2x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-7\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8130c9c9-7211-550c-a793-fbc0c25b8123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8130c9c9-7211-550c-a793-fbc0c25b8123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286807} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb267552-4b2b-5cb7-b33d-3053aabaf716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286806} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0962fd1a-afc4-5027-ba9a-43f109787f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0962fd1a-afc4-5027-ba9a-43f109787f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286809} :answer-field/id #uuid "b800bd92-2fb7-5d69-b75d-6f8284087ac8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "914f5b60-5985-590b-880c-74baeca9f21f" :question/math-academy-id "q-264788" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $9{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+3}=64\\cdot {e}^{x+3}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+3} & =64\\cdot {e}^{x+3} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+3}-64\\cdot {e}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-64)\\cdot {e}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}-64=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9{x}^{2}-64=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+8)(3x-8) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{8}{3},\\frac{8}{3}.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2604e594-c2db-5ca4-a3e7-f2fd1b952968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286812} {:answer/id #uuid "45c9199f-f6a1-59ce-8a67-593f1f9ee8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286813} {:answer/id #uuid "25a10ecd-9eff-57ea-9d31-6be7e334b429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286814} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f6292f-491f-5272-8206-0304022413ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286815} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4dc400-c99c-55ef-87bf-697f3423fe7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2604e594-c2db-5ca4-a3e7-f2fd1b952968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286812} :answer-field/id #uuid "99dc9846-2215-514f-aa35-c1bc7bec5600" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85034228-894b-54da-866f-df2a1bf5191f" :question/math-academy-id "q-88458" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+2}-7x\\cdot {3}^{x+2}+12\\cdot {3}^{x+2}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+2}-7x\\cdot {3}^{x+2}+12\\cdot {3}^{x+2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-7x+12){3}^{x+2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7x+12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{x+2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-7x+12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=3,4.$\n\n- The equation ${3}^{x+2}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{x+2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=3,4,$ and their product is $3\\cdot 4=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db5bee58-d00d-5859-a9d2-22e4681e2124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286819} {:answer/id #uuid "2e508c15-d782-54fd-920b-3262d4991c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286820} {:answer/id #uuid "19d461c6-2ac8-58b9-9d78-d72656963f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286821} {:answer/id #uuid "b39ef12a-7365-5465-bf4c-7cfa30c62e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286822} {:answer/id #uuid "e94bc761-3c26-539b-8990-63fb4d7d0fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db5bee58-d00d-5859-a9d2-22e4681e2124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286819} :answer-field/id #uuid "54727a78-44d0-583e-9e15-76d0bee6ad1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a99745ca-8714-50dc-8ff6-f259b612d828" :question/math-academy-id "q-88474" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x}=6x\\cdot {5}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x} & =6x\\cdot {5}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-6x\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-6x-45)\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6x-45=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}-6x-45=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+3)(x-5)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5.$\n\n- The equation $3=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5,$ and their product is $(-3)\\cdot 5=-15.$"}]}
